WEBVTT

00:00:00.014 --> 00:00:03.147
We now create one vector for every chunk.

00:00:03.147 --> 00:00:09.792
The code sends the chunk text to the Sentence Transformers model and asks for normalized embeddings.

00:00:09.792 --> 00:00:15.572
The resulting matrix has one row per chunk and one column per embedding dimension.

00:00:15.572 --> 00:00:20.887
With the model used here, each vector has 384 dimensions.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:24.342
Do not worry about interpreting individual numbers.

00:00:24.342 --> 00:00:27.957
A single value does not have a useful human meaning.

00:00:27.957 --> 00:00:33.082
What matters is the position of the entire vector relative to other vectors.

00:00:33.082 --> 00:00:35.697
[tts-break] The row alignment is important.

00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:41.277
Row zero in the vector matrix must correspond to row zero in the chunk table.

00:00:41.277 --> 00:00:46.597
That is how we map a similarity result back to the correct text and metadata.

00:00:46.597 --> 00:00:53.492
I recommend printing the shapes and adding an assertion that the number of vectors equals the number of

00:00:53.492 --> 00:00:57.273
chunks. Small checks like this prevent subtle retrieval bugs later.
